# Production Caddy reverse-proxy config (v1.2 Infra Migration, Phase 5, INFRA-04).
#
# {$APP_DOMAIN} is Caddy's own environment-variable placeholder syntax (not a hardcoded literal)
# -- injected by docker-compose.prod.yml's `caddy.environment.APP_DOMAIN` -- so the free-subdomain
# hostname chosen in plan 05-03 per D-01 is supplied at deploy time. A paid domain can later be
# swapped in by changing only this environment variable and the DNS A-record; no application
# change is needed (D-01's stated reversibility).
{$APP_DOMAIN} {
	# Edge rate limiting (quick task 260903-dvp) -- added ahead of the reverse_proxy line so the
	# module's own registered directive order (`before basic_auth`, caddyfile.go at the pinned
	# SHA in docker/caddy/Dockerfile) is honoured without a global options block. Both zones key
	# on {remote_host}, not {client_ip}.
	#
	# Decision, dated 2026-09-03 -- why {remote_host} and not {client_ip}: Caddy is the true edge
	# here -- no CDN, no load balancer, ports 80/443 published straight on this VM -- so the TCP
	# peer address IS the client and cannot be forged by a completed TLS handshake. {client_ip}
	# is X-Forwarded-For-derived once `trusted_proxies` is configured (it is not, anywhere in this
	# stack, today), which would make the limiter's own key attacker-supplied. Falsifier: put a
	# CDN or load balancer in front of this Caddy instance and this choice inverts -- at that
	# point {remote_host} resolves to the proxy's own address for every client, and
	# `trusted_proxies` plus {client_ip} becomes the correct pair instead.
	rate_limit {
		# Enabled deliberately, dated 2026-09-03: the module's own field comment defaults this
		# off because "keys can contain sensitive information". Here the key is the source
		# address of a public HTTPS request -- the least sensitive thing in any edge log, and
		# already the container's `json-file` log subject. Attributing a 429 after the fact is
		# otherwise impossible from inside the box. Falsifier: if this produces no output at
		# Caddy's default log level, or materially inflates the 10m x 3 rotation budget
		# (docker-compose.prod.yml's `x-logging` anchor), delete this line -- it is one token
		# and nothing else depends on it.
		log_key

		zone auth {
			match {
				path /api/signin* /api/signup*
			}
			key {remote_host}
			events 20
			window 5m
			ipv6_prefix 56
		}

		zone general {
			key {remote_host}
			events 120
			window 1m
			ipv6_prefix 56
		}
	}

	# Why the auth matcher uses a `*` suffix rather than the four exact literals it replaced
	# (2026-09-03 review, F2 -- caught before merge): the exact list `/api/signin /api/signin/
	# /api/signup /api/signup/` correctly caught every normalization variant tried against it
	# (`//api/signin`, `/api/./signin`, `%2f`, `/foo/../api/signin`) but NOT a path parameter --
	# `/api/signin;x=1` matched no literal and so spent only the general zone's budget rather than
	# the auth zone's.
	#
	# Correction, verified against the running app 2026-09-03: the first version of this note
	# claimed Spring's PathPatternParser strips path parameters, so that request "still reaches the
	# signin handler" and cost a bcrypt hash. It does not — `/api/signin;x=1` is answered 401
	# UNAUTHENTICATED by ProblemDetailAuthenticationEntryPoint, the handler is never reached, and no
	# hash is computed. So the exact-literal matcher was never the bcrypt exposure that paragraph
	# described. The `*` form is kept anyway because it is strictly safer and costs nothing, but the
	# reason is defence in depth, not a hole that was being exploited. Recorded because a wrong
	# justification in a decision record is worse than none — the next reader would trust it.
	#
	# Residual, known and accepted: `*` anchors on `/api/signin`, so a matrix parameter on an
	# EARLIER segment (`/api;a=b/signin`) still evades the auth zone. Also not exploitable — Spring
	# answers those 401 UNAUTHENTICATED without reaching the handler — and the general zone still
	# counts them. Stated so it is not re-derived from scratch.
	#
	# `*` deliberately over-matches (it also covers `/api/signinfoo`, which 404s): for a rate
	# limiter, matching slightly too much fails safe and matching too little does not. Verified
	# 2026-09-03 that only `/api/signin`, `/api/signup` and `/api/logout` live under these
	# prefixes, so nothing legitimate and high-volume is caught by the wildcard.
	#
	# Why these numbers: 20 requests per 5 minutes on the auth zone bounds sustained cost at
	# roughly 4 attempts/minute/address. Chosen over a tighter 10 (human decision, 2026-09-03)
	# because the key is an IP: an office or CGNAT pool shares one bucket, so the limit has to
	# tolerate several distinct real people failing a login at once, not just one. 120 per minute
	# on the general zone sits comfortably above this repo's own reference 54-request burst
	# (docs/INFRA_RUNBOOK.md) and above the scheduled health probe. An auth request consumes from
	# BOTH zones -- the zones compose, they do not partition -- so exhausting the auth zone also
	# spends twenty of the general zone's budget.
	#
	# What is deliberately NOT here, dated 2026-09-03: a `handle_errors 429` block, so a 429 from
	# this directive does not carry the `application/problem+json` envelope every other error on
	# this API returns -- a real, accepted inconsistency in the public contract. Left open because
	# whether the module's `Retry-After` header survives being rewritten by an error handler is
	# unproven; closing this is a follow-up with that specific proof as its acceptance criterion.
	reverse_proxy app:8080
}

# No `tls internal` directive and no `auto_https off` here, deliberately: this site block relies
# entirely on Caddy's automatic HTTPS, which obtains a real, publicly trusted Let's Encrypt
# certificate via the HTTP-01 challenge -- which is why port 80 must stay open in addition to 443
# (see docker-compose.prod.yml's caddy service). INFRA-04 requires real, publicly trusted HTTPS; a
# self-signed certificate would satisfy the letter of "HTTPS" while failing its actual purpose.
#
# Caddy's certificate/state data directory (`/data`) is mounted as a named Docker volume in
# docker-compose.prod.yml precisely so that recreating this container reuses the existing
# certificate instead of requesting a new one. Repeated certificate re-requests trigger a
# week-long Let's Encrypt rate limit -- see docker-compose.prod.yml's caddy service comment.

# Second site block, added plan 08-01 (v1.3, Phase 8, NONPROD-04). {$APP_DOMAIN_NONPROD} is
# supplied by docker-compose.prod.yml's caddy.environment.APP_DOMAIN_NONPROD -- the exact
# enumerated nonprod hostname (D-05), never a DNS wildcard pattern: DuckDNS is a shared public
# suffix, and a wildcard address here would scope this block to every other DuckDNS tenant, not
# just this project's own two subdomains. Both blocks share the one `caddy-data` volume above, so
# recreating this container reuses both certificates rather than re-requesting either.
#
# Deliberately no rate_limit directive in this block (quick task 260903-dvp): nonprod runs chatty
# e2e suites that must not be throttled, and its lack of a limit is also the negative control that
# proves the production block's directive is actually scoped to that block and not leaking global
# state -- see Task 2's local verification for the observed proof.
{$APP_DOMAIN_NONPROD} {
	reverse_proxy app-nonprod:8080
}
